'''''Asphodelus ramosus''''', the '''branched asphodel''', is a [[perennial plant|perennial]] [[herbaceous plant]] in the order [[Asparagales]]. Similar in appearance to ''[[Asphodelus albus]]'' and particularly ''[[Asphodelus cerasiferus]]'' and ''[[Asphodelus aestivus]]'', it may be distinguished by its highly branched stem and smaller fruits.<ref name="luirig.altervista.org">[http://luirig.altervista.org/flora/taxa/index1.php?scientific-name=asphodelus+ramosus Altervista Flora Italiana, ''Asphodelus ramosus''] includes photos plus European distribution map</ref><ref>[https://www.jstor.org/stable/1222883?seq=1#page_scan_tab_contents Lectotypification of Asphodelus ramosus (Asphodelaceae), a Misunderstood Linnaean Name]</ref> There has been much confusion over the nomenclature and taxonomy of the species, owing to its similarity to ''[[Asphodelus aestivus]]''.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Lifante |first1=Zoila Díaz |last2=Valdés |first2=Benito |title=Lectotypification of Asphodelus ramosus (Asphodelaceae), a Misunderstood Linnaean Name |journal=Taxon |date=1994 |volume=43 |issue=2 |pages=247–251 |doi=10.2307/1222883 |jstor=1222883}}</ref><ref name="VPG">{{citation |title=''Vascular plants of Greece: an annotated checklist''|work=[[Englera 31]] |publisher=[[Botanic Garden and Botanical Museum Berlin-Dahlem]] |url=http://portal.cybertaxonomy.org/flora-greece/intro |accessdate=2018-03-04}}</ref>

''Asphodelus ramosus'' is native to the [[Mediterranean]] region of southern [[Europe]], northern [[Africa]], and the [[Middle East]]. It can also be found in the [[Canary Islands]].<ref>[http://apps.kew.org/wcsp/namedetail.do?name_id=275588 Kew World Checklist of Selected Plant Families]</ref><ref>Dobignard, D. & Chatelain, C. (2010). Index synonymique de la flore d'Afrique du nord 1: 1-455. Éditions des conservatoire et jardin botaniques, Genève.</ref><ref>Davis, P.H. (ed.) (1984). Flora of Turkey and the East Aegean Islands 8: 1-632. Edinburgh University Press, Edinburgh.</ref><ref name="EURO_MED_ASP_AEST">{{citation |title=''The Euro+Med PlantBase''|work=[[the information resource for Euro-Mediterranean plant diversity]] |publisher=[[Botanic Garden and Botanical Museum Berlin-Dahlem]] |url=http://ww2.bgbm.org/EuroPlusMed/PTaxonDetail.asp?NameId=9024&PTRefFk=8000000 |accessdate=2018-03-04}}</ref><ref name="Plants_World_ASP_AEST">{{citation |title=''Plants of the World Online''|work=[[the reference for global plant names]] |publisher=[[Royal Botanic Gardens, Kew]] |url=https://powo.science.kew.org/taxon/urn:lsid:ipni.org:names:531446-1 |accessdate=2020-08-11}}</ref> It is particularly common in coastal areas of the Mediterranean with intensive grazing and occasional fire. In some areas it shows an affinity for acidic soils, mainly [[schist]]. It can form abundant colonies, usually flowering in February to May.<ref name="Flora_Israel">{{citation |title=''Flora of Israel Online''|work=[[Analytical Flora]] |publisher=[[The Jerusalem Botanical Gardens]] |url=https://flora.org.il/en/plants/aspram/ |accessdate=2020-08-11}}</ref><ref name="luirig.altervista.org"/> It has been thought to be [[Homer|Homer's]] asphodel of the [[underworld]],<ref>{{cite journal|last1=Anonymous|title=Homer the botanist|journal=Macmillan's Magazine|date=May–October 1887|volume=56|pages=428–436|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=uZBHAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA428|accessdate=3 November 2014|publisher=Macmillan and Company|location=London}}</ref> but so has the closely related ''[[Asphodeline lutea]]''. The branched asphodel is known to contain [[colchicine]], a chemical used in the treatment of gout.{{citation needed|date=June 2015}}

==Physical characteristics==
[[File:Asphodelus ramosus and the View from Tel Gamma, northwestern Negev, Israel (2).JPG|thumb|Branched asphodel in the [[Negev]], Israel]]

''Asphodelus ramosus'' is a [[geophyte]], having an underground storage organ which enables the plant to survive adverse conditions, such as excessive heat and drought. Its leaves, growing to a height of {{convert|60|cm}}–{{convert|80|cm}}, contain alkaloids that are harmful to sheep and goats in the wet, winter months, but during the summer when their leaves dry out, they lose their toxicity.<ref>{{cite journal |last=Rhizopoulou |first=S. |title=Ecophysiological Adaptations of Asphodelus aestivus auct. (=ramosus) to Mediterranean Climate Periodicity: Water Relations and Energetic Status |journal=Ecography |volume=20 |issue=6 |pages=626–633 |jstor=3683251 |date=1997 |doi=10.1111/j.1600-0587.1997.tb00431.x}}</ref><ref name="Avi Shmida 2005, p. 77">Avi Shmida, ''MAPA's Dictionary of Plants and Flowers in Israel'', Tel-Aviv 2005, s.v. ''Asphodelus aestivus auct.=ramosus'' (p. 77) {{ISBN|965-521-017-0}} (Hebrew)</ref> Its very numerous [[flower]]s are white with six [[tepal]]s bearing a central brown streak. The fruits are small round capsules.<ref>Danin, A. (2004). Distribution Atlas of Plants in the Flora Palaestina area: 1-517. The Israel Academy of Sciences and Humanities, Jerusalem.</ref>

==Uses==
Some traditional folk usages of the plant have been to make a glue from the plant's root. A remedy against warts is also derived from its root.<ref name="Avi Shmida 2005, p. 77"/> According to [[Dioscorides]], a concoction made from its roots (mixed with wine) induces vomiting.<ref>Dioscorides, ''[[De Materia Medica]]'' (Book II, § 199).</ref> Formerly, the entire plant was used in treating venomous snake bites (its efficacy yet to be proven scientifically).

